BY THE GRACE OF ALMIGHTY GOD

MAYOR OF TERNATE,

Considering:
a. that violence against women and children is a form of crime against humanity which constitutes a violation of human rights;
b. that the number of cases and victims of violence against women and children in Ternate City is still quite high, while services and protection have not been carried out optimally;
c. that based on the considerations as referred to in letters a and b, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation concerning the Implementation of Protection for Women and Children Victims of Violence;

In view of:
1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;

2. Law Number 7 of 1984 concerning Ratification of the Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination Against Women (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1984 Number 29, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3277);
3. Law Number 11 of 1999 concerning the Establishment of the Municipality of Second Level Region of Ternate (State Gazette of 1999 Number 45, Supplement to the State Gazette Number 3824);
4. Law Number 20 of 1999 concerning Ratification of ILO Convention Number 138 concerning Minimum Age for Admission to Employment (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 56, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3835);
5. Law Number 39 of 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3886);
6. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette Number 4235);
7. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 95);
8. Law Number 32 of 2004 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004, Number 125 Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4437) as amended several times most recently by Law Number 12 of 2008 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 32 of 2004 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2008 Number 59, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4844);
9. Law Number 33 of 2004 concerning Financial Balance between the Central Government and Regional Governments (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 126);
10. Law Number 12 of 2011 concerning the Formulation of Legislation (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2011 Number 82, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5234);
11. Law Number 36 of 2009 concerning Health (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2009 Number 12, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4967);
12. Government Regulation Number 4 of 2006 concerning the Implementation and Cooperation for Recovery of Victims of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 15, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4604);
13. Government Regulation Number 38 of 2007 concerning the Division of Governmental Affairs between the Government, Provincial Regional Governments, and Regency/City Regional Governments (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2007 Number 82, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4737);
14. Regional Regulation of Ternate City Number 19 of 2008 concerning Regional Governmental Affairs of Ternate City (Regional Gazette of Ternate City of 2008 Number 28);

With the Mutual Approval of THE REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF TERNATE CITY and

CHAPTER II

PRINCIPLES AND OBJECTIVES

Article 2

The principles of providing protection to women and children victims of violence are:
a. respect and recognition of equal human rights and dignity;
b. protection of the human rights of women and children;
c. justice and gender equality;
d. non-discrimination; dane. the best interests of victims.

CHAPTER IV

OBLIGATIONS AND RESPONSIBILITIES

Article 6

1. The Regional Government is obliged and responsible to implement efforts to prevent the occurrence of violence in the form of: a. collecting data and information about victims of violence against women and children;
b. implementing education and guidance on anti-violence and protection for women and children victims of violence;
c. implementing socialization of laws and regulations related to the provision of protection to women and children victims of violence;
d. conducting monitoring and evaluation of the provision of protection to women and children victims of violence.
2. To anticipate the occurrence of acts of violence, the Regional Government is obliged to provide and organize protection for women and children in the form of: a. establishing and facilitating the implementation of an integrated service center for victims involving community elements;
b. encouraging community awareness about the importance of protection for victims.
3. The Regional Government in carrying out its obligations as referred to in paragraphs (1) and (2) must pay attention to the rights and obligations of parents, guardians, husbands or other persons who are legally responsible for victims.

Article 9

1. The forms of services to victims organized by P2TP2A include: a. medical services, in the form of treatment and recovery of physical injuries aimed at restoring the physical condition of victims carried out by medical personnel and paramedics;
b. medicolegal services are a form of medical services for evidentiary purposes in the legal field;
c. psychosocial services are services provided by companions in order to restore the traumatic condition of victims, including the provision of safe houses to protect victims from various threats and intimidation as well as providing social support so that victims have self-confidence, strength and independence in resolving their problems;
d. legal services to assist victims in undergoing the judicial process;
e. economic independence services in the form of skills training and provision of economic access so that victims can live independently.
2. The protection mechanism is organized based on Standard Operating Procedures (SOP) which will be further regulated by Mayor Regulation.
